Revision history for MCP::Server::Tool::FileSystem

{{$NEXT}}

0.2.4  2026-10-24T19:29:01+01:00
    - HOTFIX: [2026-10-24] Windows filesystem testing checks for errors on
      POSIX reqs.

0.2.3  2026-10-24T18:51:14+01:00
    - BUGFIX: [2026-10-24] `grep` believes an external searcher's file list
      only when the searcher's own documentation calls its exit an answer,
      and gives a searcher up only when it is gone or keeps failing. A
      searcher killed by a signal part-way through its list -- which Rakudo
      reports as exit code 0 -- had its partial list believed, and the files
      it never got to were silently missing from the answer; a list with a
      signal, or with any exit status but 0, 1, or 2 with nothing on stderr,
      now costs that search a full scan. Exit 2 with a silent stderr is what
      ripgrep, GNU grep and BSD grep all do, told to keep quiet about
      unreadable files, when the root holds one: it lists every file they
      could read, so it is now believed, where before one unreadable file
      demoted the searcher for the rest of the process. Exit 2 with a
      message (an unknown option, memory exhausted) is a refusal and is not
      believed. One answer that cannot be believed no longer demotes the
      searcher; five in a row, with no believed answer between them, drop
      it, and a believed answer starts the count again. A searcher whose
      binary has gone since its probe is dropped by the first search that
      misses it, so the hundreds after it spawn nothing; one that is still
      there but could not be launched (out of file descriptors, out of
      processes) is tried again by the next search, and that never counts
      against it. The probe likewise no longer remembers a "no" it never
      got: a binary that is there but could not be launched, or was killed
      before it replied, is asked again on the next call, while a binary
      that is not there, or that ran and gave no version, is remembered as
      before. New public `MCP::Server::Tool::FileSystem::reset-searchers()`
      forgets all of it -- verdicts, drops and counts -- so the next search
      probes from scratch: a harness can bind it to a command for a user who
      has just installed ripgrep, or whose searcher was dropped in error.
      New t/08 subtests drive stand-in searchers the test writes for itself
      through each case, including resets during concurrent searches, and
      t/07 runs every installed tier against a root holding a file nobody
      may read.
    - BUGFIX: [2026-10-24] a long-running server on macOS no longer runs out
      of file descriptors. Rakudo 2026.09 builds `$*DISTRO` afresh on every
      read (it files the object it built under a key its lookup never asks
      for; rakudo/rakudo a22cab8f9), and on macOS each build runs `sw_vers`
      and leaves a pipe to it open. This pack reads `$*DISTRO` for every path
      component it handles, so each `fs_read` kept a dozen descriptors and a
      search at least one per file it walked: at macOS's default limit of 256
      a server lasted a few dozen calls before ripgrep could not be started
      -- and was demoted to the Raku scan for the rest of the process -- and
      soon after that no file could be opened at all. The pack now files the
      `$*DISTRO` Rakudo built under the key the lookup reads, once, when it
      loads: nothing changes on a release without the bug, and on one with
      it every path operation stops costing a process spawn. And a searcher
      that passed its probe but then cannot be started at all -- out of
      descriptors, out of processes, its binary being rewritten by an upgrade
      -- is no longer demoted for good: that search scans everything and the
      next one tries again. New t/11-handles.rakutest counts the handles the
      process holds open (the kernel's descriptor list on Linux and macOS,
      GetProcessHandleCount on Windows) around a few hundred calls of every
      tool and a hundred searches in a row; a new t/08 subtest pins the
      retry. And t/08 no longer dies on a host with no system grep, where
      its skip was a `return` outside any routine and failed the install.
    - SECURITY: [2026-10-01] on Windows a path segment that would open
      some other name than it spells is refused: one holding a `:` (a data
      stream -- link::$INDEX_ALLOCATION is the directory `link` itself,
      whatever kind of link it is), one ending in a dot or a space (Win32
      drops them, so secret. IS secret), and one shaped like an 8.3 short
      name (SECRET~1, PROGRA~1.TXT, a Samba server's SECRE~A1). None of
      them leaves the root, but each let the name a permission layer in
      front of the pack judged differ from the name the OS opened: a client
      rule denying C:\work\secret was walked past by SECRET~1\x, which
      Windows opens as secret\x. The file's full name always works; a file
      whose own long name has the short-name shape cannot be reached on
      Windows. POSIX is unchanged: all of these are ordinary names there.
      New t/03 subtest pins the rule on every host, and the resolver's use
      of it under a faked $*DISTRO.
    - SECURITY: [2026-10-01] the device names refused on every platform are
      the whole set Windows reserves: CONIN$, CONOUT$, COM0, LPT0 and the
      superscripts COM¹-³ and LPT¹-³ join CON, PRN, AUX, NUL and COM1-9 /
      LPT1-9, and the name is compared as Windows compares it -- before the
      first dot, trailing spaces dropped -- so `NUL .txt` is refused too.
      t/03 pins them.
    - MAINT: [2026-10-01] moved the file-time test support from
      `t/lib/FileTimeFixture.rakumod` into
      `lib/MCP/Server/Tool/FileSystem/Test/FileTimeFixture.rakumod`
      (`MCP::Server::Tool::FileSystem::Test::FileTimeFixture`), declared in
      `provides`, per the fleet-wide convention: a `t/lib` directory is its
      own CompUnit repo that starts cold on a fresh `zef install`, and
      parallel test files pulling a fixture's dependency chain into that
      cold store can race and fail the install outright.
    - SECURITY: [2026-10-01] containment on Windows resolves `..` the way
      Windows does. The symlink chase popped `..` against wherever the walk
      had really got to, which is the POSIX rule; Windows collapses `..` by
      name before the filesystem sees a path, and joins a relative link
      target onto the link's directory and collapses it the same way. So a
      link planted inside the root -- evil.txt -> L\..\..\secret.txt, with L
      a link two levels deeper -- was judged to stay inside while Windows
      opened a file outside the root, and fs_read returned it. Planting one
      needs a symbolic link inside the root (Developer Mode, an elevated
      process, or a checkout of a repository that carries one). The chase
      now applies each platform's own rule; POSIX is unchanged. On Windows
      it also refuses a device-path link target (\\?\..., \\.\...) and a
      path segment made of dots and spaces other than `.` and `..`, which
      Win32 would rename on the way in.
    - CHANGE: [2026-10-01] `physical-path` is public API, documented under
      "Resolving a path the way the OS will". It is what any code naming a
      directory for the OS to check should use in place of IO::Path.resolve,
      which does not follow symlinks on Windows and mis-joins a junction's
      target onto the junction's parent. App::Sadna's sandbox profiles are
      the first outside user. New t/10-physical-path.rakutest reads every
      case through the OS and checks the resolver names the file the OS
      opened; directory links are junctions on Windows, so it needs no
      privilege there.
    - BUGFIX: [2026-10-01] `stat` reports the right modification time. It
      read IO::Path.modified, which on 64-bit Windows puts every file
      within about two seconds of 1970 (MoarVM builds the nanosecond
      timestamp by multiplying libuv's `long` seconds before widening, and
      `long` is 32 bits there) and everywhere else is 37 seconds early (the
      leap seconds are never added). It now reads the floating-point stat
      time, which is right on every platform, and so does the map cache's
      freshness key. t/02 sets a file's time outright and checks `stat`
      reports exactly it.
    - CHANGE: [2026-09-19] the published input schema now advertises every
      default the handlers apply: `path = '.'` on list, glob, grep and map,
      `regex = false` and `glob = ''` and `context = 0` and
      `max-results = 50` on grep, `query = ''` and `focus = ''` and
      `max-chars = 8192` on map, `replace-all = false` on edit, and
      `recursive = false` on delete. The signatures in the Pod always said so;
      the schema the model actually reads did not, so a call that omitted
      `path` searched the whole root and looked to the model like an answer.
      No handler changed -- `path` is still optional and still defaults to the
      root -- and the new t/09-schema-contract.rakutest pins every claim
      against the handler: each required parameter is called with that
      parameter missing and has to be refused, and each declared default is
      checked by calling the tool with the argument omitted and with the
      declared value, and demanding the same answer both times.
      (The defaults reach the wire with the MCP::Server release that
      carries a param spec's `default` into the emitted schema; against an
      older one they are simply not published.)
    - FIX: [2026-09-19] concurrent fs_grep calls could kill the server
      process outright. Several recursive Raku scans at once reliably tripped
      a garbage-collector fault in MoarVM 2026.07 ("Heap corruption detected:
      pointer ... to past fromspace"), which prints one line and calls exit():
      no END block, no LEAVE, no signal handler, nothing at the Raku level
      that could catch it. Measured at six concurrent searches, grep was the
      only tool in the pack heavy enough to trigger it, and it failed 6 runs
      out of 6. It now takes both of the measures that measured clean:

      The scanning phase of every search runs under one PROCESS-WIDE lock
      (0/6). Process-wide rather than per-instance, because the fault is in
      the collector and does not care how many toolkits there are.

      And when ripgrep or a system grep is installed, that tool is asked which
      files contain the pattern and the scan opens only those (0/6) -- which
      removes the allocation volume the collector was choking on. The call to
      the searcher is made OUTSIDE the lock, so those stay parallel.

      The external tool chooses candidate files and nothing else: it never
      produces a line of the answer, never decides the order, and never sees
      the glob, context or max-results. Every byte a model reads still comes
      from the same scanner over the same walk, which is what makes the tiers
      identical by construction rather than by careful formatting. A regex
      search never leaves Raku (the pattern is a RAKU regex, safe only because
      Rakudo refuses to interpolate one carrying a code block; ripgrep would
      read it as Rust syntax and grep as POSIX), and neither does a pattern
      outside printable ASCII (Raku searches NFG text, a searcher searches
      bytes) or a search of a single file. A host with neither tool -- a stock
      Windows, a minimal container -- is served by the Raku scan exactly as
      before, now serialised.
    - CHANGE: [2026-09-19] grep no longer ends an answer with
      "[skipped N files that are not UTF-8 text]". Ripgrep passes a binary
      file over in silence and cannot be made to count them, so that notice
      was the one part of the output that could not survive the question
      "which tier served this call?". Binary and mis-encoded files are still
      skipped, on the same NUL sniff and the same decode as before; they are
      simply not counted. "[stopped at N matching lines; raise max-results to
      see more]" is untouched -- every tier reproduces it.
    - NEW: [2026-09-19] MCP_FS_GREP_BACKEND pins grep to a tier (auto,
      ripgrep, grep or raku), and MCP_FS_GREP_RG_BINARY /
      MCP_FS_GREP_GREP_BINARY say which binary each tier spawns. They exist
      for t/07-grep-parity.rakutest, which runs every kind of query through
      every searcher installed on the machine and asserts the answers are
      identical strings; they are also the escape hatch on a host whose
      ripgrep is installed under another name. A tier is probed at most once
      per binary per process -- the "no" cached as firmly as the "yes" -- and
      a searcher that fails at work is demoted for the life of the process,
      with that search falling back and answering normally.
    - MAINT: [2026-08-27] moved the shared test fixtures from
      C<t/lib/FSTest.rakumod> into
      C<lib/MCP/Server/Tool/FileSystem/Test/Kit.rakumod>
      (C<MCP::Server::Tool::FileSystem::Test::Kit>). A C<t/lib>
      directory is its own CompUnit repo that starts cold on a fresh
      C<zef install>, and parallel test files pulling the fixture's
      dependency chain into that cold store can race and fail the
      install outright. C<lib/> is staged and precompiled before the
      test phase runs, so shipping test support inside the dist's own
      namespace dissolves the race. This is the fleet-wide convention
      going forward.

0.2.2  2026-08-21T21:05:33+01:00
    - Require MCP::Server 0.6.0, the release that actually ships what 0.2.1
      leans on: tool annotations in the tools/list catalog, the annotated-batch
      concurrent scheduler, and the bridge's content-only rendering. The old
      0.4.0+ pin let a resolver hand this pack a server that silently dropped
      its annotations.

0.2.1  2026-08-21T20:36:03+01:00
    - The six reading tools (read, list, glob, stat, grep, map) now declare the
      MCP annotations readOnlyHint and idempotentHint, so a host may run a
      batch of them side by side and execute identical calls once. The claim
      is about the handlers as much as the intent: the observation tools take
      this instance's mutation lock and the map cache is behind its own, so
      several reads on ONE pack instance see coherent state. The five mutating
      tools deliberately declare nothing.

0.2.0  2026-08-20T22:49:48+01:00
    - New reading tool: map(path, query, focus, max-chars). Returns a ranked
      skeleton of the code below a directory — each file as its path plus the
      definition lines of its most-referenced symbols, never bodies — so a
      model can orient in an unfamiliar tree with one call instead of a dozen
      speculative reads. Ranking is a personalized PageRank over the
      "file A refers to something file B defines" graph; query re-centres it on
      matching identifiers and focus on named paths. Registered on read-only
      kits too.
    - map understands C, C++, Go, Java, JavaScript, Python, Rust, TypeScript
      and TSX through TreeSitter::Native, and Raku through a heuristic scanner
      (no tree-sitter grammar for Raku exists), where `use Foo::Bar` resolves
      to the file that is Foo::Bar. The pack ships supplemental tree-sitter
      queries for C and C++ (whose vendored tags.scm files tag no references at
      all) and for TypeScript/TSX (whose vendored file tags only signature
      declarations, missing every function with a body).
    - map picks a file's representative definitions by how much the tree refers
      to them, sharing a name's references among the files that define it and
      setting aside names most of the map refers to. Matched-by-name references
      cannot tell whose `new` they mean, and counting them naively made
      "method new" the single line printed for three unrelated files.
    - map's Raku scanner skips declarator documentation in every form, the
      bracketed `#|( ... )` and `#=( ... )` blocks included. Their continuation
      lines carry no `#`, so prose about code — which says "sub", "class" and
      "constant" constantly — was being read as code and could outrank the
      declarations around it.
    - map bounds itself: prunes .git, node_modules, build output and every
      dot-directory; skips files over 1MB, binaries and unreadable files, and
      counts them; stops after 5000 files; and fits its answer into max-chars
      by dropping whole files rather than truncating one. Parse results are
      cached per file on modification time and size, so mapping, editing one
      file and mapping again re-reads only that file.
    - New dependency: TreeSitter::Native.
    - Tool results now include structured `sha256-v1` revisions while preserving
      their existing text. Files hash exact bytes; directories hash a canonical
      recursive tree; absent paths have a stable token.
    - SECURITY: write, edit, mkdir, move and delete accept the unadvertised
      `_expected-revisions` condition. Compare and mutation share one provider
      lock, stale conditions perform no mutation, and concurrent conditional
      writers cannot both win.

0.1.1  2026-08-09T20:26:49+01:00
    - SECURITY: symlink containment now holds on Windows. The defence relied
      on IO::Path.resolve, which does not follow symlinks there, so a link
      planted in the sandbox could be read and written straight through
      (caught by the Windows CI lane). Containment now chases links itself
      (physical-path, realpath-style over readlink, hop budget against
      cycles, fail-closed on unreadable or drive-relative targets) — one
      code path on every platform. path-contains unifies separator spelling
      on Windows; the root itself is chased at construction. New containment
      tests: chains, mid-path directory links, relative targets (in-root
      '..' targets still read through), cycles.

0.1.0  2026-08-09T20:00:53+01:00
    - Initial release
    - MCP::Server::Tool::FileSystem: a root-confined filesystem toolkit that
      plugs into any MCP::Server via $server.plug($kit) or
      MCP::Server.new(:tools['FileSystem' => { root => '...' }]).
    - Tools (default prefix "fs"): read, write, edit, list, glob, grep, stat,
      mkdir, move, delete. A read-only kit registers only the five reading
      tools — read, list, glob, grep and stat — and never advertises the rest.
    - edit(path, old-string, new-string, replace-all): exact-match replacement
      with no fuzzy matching. Zero matches, an ambiguous match without
      replace-all, an empty old-string and an old-string equal to new-string
      are all refused; the confirmation counts occurrences and bytes.
    - read gained optional offset (1-based) and limit. Omitting both returns
      the file byte for byte as before; a range comes back numbered "N<tab>text"
      with a "[lines A-B of N total]" footer, and reading past the end is an
      answer rather than an error.
    - grep(pattern, path, regex, glob, context, max-results): GNU-grep-shaped
      output ("path:line:text", "-" for context lines, "--" between groups).
      Literal by default; regex mode is a native Raku regex, and a pattern
      carrying a code block is refused by Rakudo's interpolation guard rather
      than executed. Binary and non-UTF-8 files are skipped and counted, and
      the search stops at max-results with a notice.
    - move(from, to) and delete(path, recursive). Both ends of a move go
      through the resolver; an existing destination, a missing destination
      parent, and moving or deleting the root itself are all refused. A
      recursive delete unlinks symlinks instead of following them.
    - Files are read as bytes and decoded once rather than slurped as text:
      Raku's text-mode slurp folds CRLF into LF and spurt never puts it back,
      which would have made every edit of a CRLF file rewrite the lines it did
      not touch. read is now verbatim in the sense edit needs it to be.
    - Parameter naming is a documented contract: location parameters are
      exactly "path", "from" and "to", and multi-word parameters are
      kebab-case, so a permission layer in front of the server can find the
      paths a call would touch by name.
    - Containment: every path parameter must be relative, may not contain ".."
      and may not name a Windows device; the nearest existing ancestor is
      resolved and checked against the root by parent-chain walk, so symlinks
      planted inside the sandbox cannot be used as a door out of it.
    - glob is a portable, regex-free matcher: "*" matches within one path
      segment, "?" matches one character, nothing else is special.
